{{Infobox fluorine}}
'''Fluorine''' is a [[chemical element]]; it has the [[Chemical symbol|symbol]] '''F''' and [[atomic number]] 9. It is the lightest [[halogen]] and exists at [[Standard temperature and pressure|standard conditions]] as pale yellow [[Diatomic molecule|diatomic]] gas. Fluorine is extremely [[Reactivity (chemistry)|reactive]] as it reacts with all other [[Periodic table|elements]] except for the light [[noble gas]]es. Fluorine in its elemental form is highly [[toxicity|toxic]].

Among the elements, fluorine ranks [[Abundance of the chemical elements|24th in cosmic abundance and 13th in crustal abundance]]. [[Fluorite]], the primary mineral source of fluorine, which gave the element its name, was first described in 1529; as it was added to metal [[ore]]s to lower their melting points for [[smelting]], the Latin verb {{lang|la|fluo}} meaning {{gloss|to flow}} gave the mineral its name. Proposed as an element in 1810, fluorine proved difficult and dangerous to separate from its compounds, and several early experimenters died or sustained injuries from their attempts. Only in 1886 did French chemist [[Henri Moissan]] isolate elemental fluorine using low-temperature [[electrolysis]], a process still employed for modern production. Industrial production of fluorine gas for [[Enriched uranium#Enrichment methods|uranium enrichment]], its largest application, began during the [[Manhattan Project]] in [[World War&nbsp;II]].

Owing to the expense of refining pure fluorine, most commercial applications use fluorine compounds, with about half of mined fluorite used in [[steelmaking]]. The rest of the fluorite is converted into [[hydrogen fluoride]] en route to various organic fluorides, or into [[cryolite]], which plays a key role in [[Hall–Héroult process|aluminium refining]]. The [[carbon–fluorine bond]] is usually very stable. Organofluorine compounds are widely used as [[refrigerant]]s, electrical insulation, and [[Polytetrafluoroethylene|PTFE]] (Teflon). Pharmaceuticals such as [[atorvastatin]] and [[fluoxetine]] contain C−F bonds. The [[Fluoride|fluoride ion]] from dissolved fluoride salts inhibits dental cavities and so finds use in [[toothpaste]] and [[water fluoridation]]. [[Fluorochemical industry|Global fluorochemical]] sales amount to more than [[United States dollar|US$]]15&nbsp;billion a year.

[[Fluorocarbon]] gases are generally [[greenhouse gas]]es with [[Global warming potential|global-warming potentials]] 100 to 23,500 times that of [[carbon dioxide]], and {{chem2|SF6|link=Sulfur hexafluoride}} has the highest global warming potential of any known substance. [[Organofluorine chemistry|Organofluorine compounds]] often persist in the environment due to the strength of the carbon–fluorine bond. Fluorine has no known metabolic role in mammals, but a few [[plant]]s and [[Sponge|marine sponges]] synthesize organofluorine poisons (most often [[Fluoroacetic acid|monofluoroacetates]]) that help deter predation.{{sfn|Lee et al.|2014}}
