[[Honorific]] nicknames are used when describing [[popular music]] artists, most often in the media or by fans, to indicate their significance, and are often [[Pantheon (religion)|religious]], [[Kinship terminology|familial]], or most frequently [[Imperial, royal and noble ranks|royal and aristocratic]] titles, used [[metaphor]]ically. Honorific nicknames were used in classical music in [[Europe]] even in the early 19th century, with figures such as [[Mozart]] being called "The father of modern music" and [[Bach]] "The father of modern piano music".<ref>{{cite book |last=Frey |first=Albert R. |title=Sobriquets and Nicknames |publisher=Ticknor and Company |location=Boston |year=1887 |page=115 |url=https://archive.org/details/sobriquetsnickna00frey}}</ref> They were also particularly prominent in [[African-American culture]] in the post-[[American Civil War|Civil War]] era, perhaps as a means of conferring status that had been negated by [[Slavery in the United States|slavery]],<ref>{{cite journal |last=Walker |first=Sheila S. |date=June 1977 |title=What's in a name: Black awareness keeps the African tradition of 'meaningful names' alive |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2csDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA74 |journal=[[Ebony (magazine)|Ebony]] |volume=32 |issue=8 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230713152059/https://books.google.com/books?id=2csDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA74 |archive-date=July 13, 2023 |access-date=August 30, 2026 |pages=74–84}}</ref> and as a result entered early jazz and blues music, including figures such as [[Duke Ellington]] and [[Count Basie]].<ref name="EvansD_2008">{{cite book |last=Evans |first=David |chapter=From Bumble Bee Slim to Black Boy Shine: Nicknames of Blues Singers |editor-last=Evans |editor-first=David |title=Ramblin' on My Mind: New Perspectives on the Blues |publisher=University of Illinois Press |year=2008 |pages=179–221 |isbn=978-0-252-03203-5}}</ref>

In [[Culture of the United States|U.S. culture]], despite [[Republicanism in the United States|its republican constitution and ideology]],<ref>{{cite book |last1=Deger-Jalkotzy |first1=Sigrid |last2=Lemos |first2=I. S. |author-link1=Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy |title=Ancient Greece: from the Mycenaean palaces to the age of Homer |location=Edinburgh |publisher=[[Edinburgh University Press]] |year=2006 |page=272}}</ref> royal honorific nicknames have been used to describe leading figures in various areas of activity, such as industry, commerce, sports, and the media; ''[[father]]'' or ''[[mother]]'' have been used for [[innovators]], and royal titles such as ''[[king]]'' and ''queen'' for dominant figures in a field.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Benoliel |first1=M. |last2=Cashdan |first2=Linda |title=The Upper Hand: Winning Strategies from World-Class Negotiators |publisher=[[Adams Media]] |year=2006 |page=26}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Rader |first=B. G. |title=Baseball: a history of America's game |location=Chicago |publisher=[[University of Illinois Press]] |edition=3rd |year=2008 |page=140}}</ref> In the 1930s and 1940s, as jazz and swing music were gaining popularity, it was the more commercially successful white artists [[Paul Whiteman]] and [[Benny Goodman]] who became known as "the King of Jazz" and "the King of Swing" respectively, despite there being more highly regarded contemporary African-American artists.<ref name=Bynoe200p155>{{cite book |last=Bynoe |first=Y. |title=Stand and deliver: political activism, leadership, and hip hop culture |publisher=Soft Skull Press |year=2004 |page=155}}</ref>

These patterns of naming were transferred to rock and roll when it emerged in the 1950s. There was a series of attempts to find—and a number of claimants to be—the "King of Rock 'n' Roll", a title that became most associated with [[Elvis Presley]].<ref name="T. Bertrand, 2000 p. 220">{{cite book |last=Bertrand |first=M. T. |title=Race, Rock, and Elvis: Music in American Life |location=Chicago |publisher=[[University of Illinois Press]] |year=2000 |page=220}}</ref> This has been characterized as part of a process of the appropriation of credit for innovation of the then-new music by a white establishment.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Rodman |first=G. B. |title=A hero to most?; Elvis, myth and the politics of race |journal=[[Cultural Studies (journal)|Cultural Studies]] |volume=8 |issue=3 |year=1994 |page=474}}</ref> Different honorifics have been taken or given for other leading figures in the genre, such as "the Architect of Rock and Roll", by [[Little Richard]] from the 1990s;<ref>{{cite book |last=Warner |first=J. |title=On this day in black music history |publisher=[[Hal Leonard Corporation]] |year=2006 |page=56}}</ref> this term, like many, is also used for other important figures, in this case including pioneer electric guitarist [[Les Paul]].<ref name="rockhallLesPaul">{{cite web |title=Les Paul |url=http://www.rockhall.com/inductees/les-paul |publisher=Rock and Roll Hall of Fame Museum |access-date=June 21, 2014 |archive-date=March 17, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100317150128/http://www.rockhall.com/inductees/les-paul |url-status=live}}</ref>

Similar honorific nicknames have been given in other genres, including [[Aretha Franklin]], who was crowned the "Queen of Soul" on stage by disk jockey [[Pervis Spann]] in 1968.<ref>{{cite book |last=Kempton |first=A. |title=Boogaloo: the Quintessence of American Popular Music |publisher=[[University of Michigan Press]] |year=2005 |page=58}}</ref> [[Michael Jackson]] and [[Madonna]] have been closely associated with the terms "King of Pop" and "Queen of Pop" respectively, since the 1980s.<ref>{{citation|first=Alison|last=Hawes|title=Music and Fashion|publisher=Badger Learning|year=2014|page=23|isbn=9781781477588|quote=Many of the most successful pop artists of the Eighties were solo singers. Two of them, Michael Jackson and Madonna, were known as the King and Queen of Pop}}</ref><ref>{{cite book  |last1=Jones |first1=Bob |last2=Brown |first2=Stacy |title=Michael Jackson: The Man Behind the Mask: An Insider's Story of the King of Pop |publisher=Select Books |location=New York |year=2005 |page=49}}</ref><ref name="madonnatimes">{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/sunday-times-rich-list/profile/article/madonna-more-clout-than-the-beatles-all-by-herself-and-wearing-heels-bjcfx9mbkx7|title=Madonna: more clout than the Beatles, all by herself&nbsp;... and wearing heels|work=[[The Times]]|first=Caitlin|last=Moran|date=April 22, 2008|access-date=April 17, 2013|quote=she has been referred to habitually as "the Queen of Pop" since the mid-Eighties|archive-date=August 5, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200805103333/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/madonna-more-clout-than-the-beatles-all-by-herself-and-wearing-heels-bjcfx9mbkx7|url-status=live}}</ref> Some nicknames have been strongly promulgated and contested by various artists,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nme.com/news/tabloid-hell/34352 |title=The 'Queen of Soul' is not happy about Grammy incident |publisher=NME News |access-date=May 20, 2017 |archive-date=February 16, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080216165713/https://www.nme.com/news/tabloid-hell/34352 |url-status=live }}</ref> and occasionally disowned or played down by their subjects.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/magazine/7829013.stm |title=What's in a nickname? |date=January 15, 2009 |publisher=BBC Magazine |access-date=May 20, 2017 |archive-date=January 18, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090118223753/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/magazine/7829013.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Some notable honorific nicknames are in general usage and commonly identified with particular individuals.

