[[File:Ionic bonding.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|right|300px|Electron transfer from a neutral [[lithium]] (Li) atom on the left to a neutral [[fluorine]] (F) atom on the right would give  {{chem2|link=Lithium ion|Li+}} and  {{chem2|link=Fluoride ion|F-}} ions.]]

An '''ion''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|aɪ|.|ɒ|n|,_|-|ən}})<ref>{{cite Collins Dictionary|ion}} {{Cite web |title=Collins English Dictionary &#124; Always Free Online |url=http://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/ion |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131224095801/http://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/ion |archive-date=24 December 2013 |access-date=21 December 2013}}.</ref> is an [[atom]] or [[molecule]] with a net [[electrical charge]]. The charge of an [[electron]] is considered to be negative by convention and this charge is equal and opposite to the charge of a [[proton]], which is considered to be positive by convention. The net charge of an ion is not zero because its total number of electrons is unequal to its total number of protons.

A '''cation''' is a positively charged ion with fewer electrons than protons<ref>{{cite Merriam-Webster|cation |access-date=6 October 2021}} {{Cite web |title=Definition of CATION |url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cation |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006215047/https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cation |archive-date=6 October 2021 |access-date=6 October 2021}}.</ref> (e.g. {{chem2|K+}} ([[potassium ion]])) while an '''anion''' is a negatively charged ion with more electrons than protons<ref>{{cite Merriam-Webster|anion |access-date=6 October 2021}} {{Cite web |title=Definition of ANION |url=https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/anion |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006215049/https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/anion |archive-date=6 October 2021 |access-date=6 October 2021}}.</ref> (e.g.  {{chem2|Cl-}} ([[chloride ion]]) and  {{chem2|OH-}} ([[hydroxide ion]])). Opposite electric charges are pulled towards one another by [[electrostatic force]], so cations and anions attract each other and readily form [[ionic compound]]s. Ions consisting of only a single atom are termed ''monatomic ions'', ''atomic ions'' or ''simple ions'', while ions consisting of two or more atoms are termed [[polyatomic ion]]s or ''molecular ions''.

The net charge of an ion is indicated as a superscript on the right. To indicate a +1 or −1 charge, only a + or − is written respectively, as seen in {{chem2|Na+}} ([[sodium ion]]) and {{Chem2|F−}} ([[fluoride ion]]). To indicate a more severe charge, the number of additional or missing electrons is supplied, as seen in {{chem2|O_{2}(2-)}} ([[peroxide]], negatively charged, polyatomic) and  {{chem2|He(2+)}} ([[alpha particle]], positively charged, monatomic).<ref name="ThoughtCo">{{Cite web |title=What Is an Ion? Definition and Examples |url=https://www.thoughtco.com/definition-of-ion-604535 |access-date=26 August 2024 |website=ThoughtCo |language=en |archive-date=26 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240826143201/https://www.thoughtco.com/definition-of-ion-604535 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the case of physical ionization in a fluid (gas or liquid), "ion pairs" are created by spontaneous molecule collisions, where each generated pair consists of a free electron and a positive ion.<ref name="Knoll-1999">{{Cite book |last=Knoll |first=Glenn F. |title=Radiation Detection and Measurement |date=1999 |publisher=[[Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]] |isbn=978-0-471-07338-3 |edition=3rd |location=New York}}</ref> Ions are also created by other means, such as passing a [[direct current]] through a conducting solution, dissolving an [[anode]] via [[ionization]]. Ions in a solid ionic lattice can be released into solution by the process of [[dissolution (chemistry)|dissolution]] of a [[salt (chemistry)|salt]] in a suitable solvent liquid, often water.

